<%# app/views/tools/connection_pool_calculator.html.erb %>
<%#
  100% client-side. No network calls. Dark + Tailwind, house palette (emerald/clay).
  Targets "sidekiq connection pool limits" / "rails puma database pool".
%>

<main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-14">
  <header class="mb-8">
    <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-emerald-400">Developer Tools</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-100">Sidekiq &amp; Puma Connection Pool Calculator</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-zinc-400">
      Size your Rails database pool against Puma threads and Sidekiq concurrency —
      and see before you deploy whether you'll hit
      <code class="rounded bg-zinc-800 px-1 py-0.5 text-sm text-zinc-300">ActiveRecord::ConnectionTimeoutError</code>
      or exhaust Postgres connections.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- Calculator -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-900/40 p-6">
    <div class="grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <% [
        ["Puma workers (processes)", "puma_workers", 2],
        ["Puma threads per worker",  "puma_threads", 5],
        ["Sidekiq processes",        "sidekiq_processes", 1],
        ["Sidekiq concurrency (threads)", "sidekiq_concurrency", 10],
        ["DB pool size (database.yml)", "db_pool", 5],
        ["Postgres max_connections", "db_max", 100]
      ].each do |label, id, default| %>
        <label class="block">
          <span class="text-sm text-zinc-400"><%= label %></span>
          <input type="number" min="1" id="<%= id %>" value="<%= default %>"
                 class="mt-1 w-full rounded-lg border border-zinc-700 bg-zinc-950 px-3 py-2 text-zinc-100 focus:border-emerald-500 focus:outline-none" />
        </label>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <!-- Live verdict -->
    <div id="verdict" class="mt-6 rounded-lg border p-4 text-sm"></div>

    <!-- Recommendation + copy -->
    <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-zinc-950 p-4">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
        <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-500">Recommended config/database.yml</span>
        <button id="copyBtn" class="rounded bg-zinc-800 px-3 py-1 text-xs text-zinc-300 hover:bg-zinc-700">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre id="snippet" class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto font-mono text-xs text-emerald-300"></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Funnel links -->
  <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm">
    <a href="/blog/why-background-jobs-are-slow" class="text-emerald-400 hover:underline">Why this happens &rarr;</a>
    <a href="/services" class="text-emerald-400 hover:underline">Struggling with this in production? &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <!-- Below-the-fold SEO content (this is what ranks) -->
  <article class="prose prose-invert mt-14 max-w-none">
    <h2>How to size your Sidekiq connection pool</h2>
    <p>
      Every thread that runs a database query needs its own connection from the Rails pool.
      In a Rails app that means two separate pools to reason about: your web process (Puma)
      and your worker process (Sidekiq). The pool size is set per process in
      <code>config/database.yml</code>, and the single most common production error —
      <em>could not obtain a connection from the pool</em> — happens when a process runs
      more threads than its pool has connections.
    </p>

    <h2>The rule: pool size must cover your threads</h2>
    <p>
      For the web process, your pool must be at least your Puma thread count. For the worker
      process, your pool must be at least your Sidekiq concurrency. On Solid Queue the same
      idea applies, with the guidance to keep worker threads at or below your pool size minus
      two — two connections are reserved for the queue's own polling and heartbeat. A good
      default is to set the pool to your thread count plus a small amount of headroom.
    </p>

    <h2>Don't exceed Postgres max_connections</h2>
    <p>
      The second failure mode is global: the sum of all connections across every Puma worker
      and every Sidekiq process can exceed Postgres's <code>max_connections</code> ceiling.
      When that happens, new connection attempts are refused database-wide. If your peak
      total approaches that ceiling, the usual fix is a connection pooler like PgBouncer in
      front of Postgres, or lowering per-process concurrency.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>How many connections does Sidekiq need?</h3>
    <p>
      One per running worker thread. If Sidekiq concurrency is 10, its process needs a pool
      of at least 10 (plus a little headroom) so no job stalls waiting to check out a connection.
    </p>
    <h3>Should Puma and Sidekiq share the same pool size?</h3>
    <p>
      No. They're separate processes with separate pools. Size each to its own thread count —
      Puma threads for the web pool, Sidekiq concurrency for the worker pool — which is often
      different numbers.
    </p>
    <h3>What causes ActiveRecord::ConnectionTimeoutError?</h3>
    <p>
      A thread asked the pool for a connection and none was free within the timeout. Almost
      always it means the pool is smaller than the number of threads contending for it —
      a configuration issue, not a database fault.
    </p>
  </article>
</main>

<%# FAQ schema for the content above %>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many connections does Sidekiq need?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "One per running worker thread. If Sidekiq concurrency is 10, its process needs a pool of at least 10 plus a little headroom so no job stalls waiting to check out a connection." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should Puma and Sidekiq share the same pool size?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. They are separate processes with separate pools. Size each to its own thread count: Puma threads for the web pool, Sidekiq concurrency for the worker pool." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What causes ActiveRecord::ConnectionTimeoutError?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "A thread asked the pool for a connection and none was free within the timeout. It almost always means the pool is smaller than the number of threads contending for it, which is a configuration issue rather than a database fault." }
